## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Stack growth is a core OS feature. This question tests understanding of page faults, per-thread state, synchronization, and safe growth under concurrent faults in a minimal kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Guard pages and dynamic stack growth
- Per-thread stack boundaries (stack_base, stack_limit)
- Atomic/locked updates to stack metadata
- Race conditions when multiple faults occur near the guard
- Basic testing: concurrent faults, growth cap, and resume correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-skeleton: handle_stack_fault in a toy OS
void handle_stack_fault(Thread *t, void *fault_addr) {
  if (!in_stack_range(t, fault_addr)) return; // not stack fault
  acquire(&t->stack_lock);
  if (t->stack_limit - t->stack_base < GROW_SIZE) {
    release(&t->stack_lock);
    // terminate or signal OOM
    return;
  }
  map_pages(t, t->stack_limit - GROW_SIZE, GROW_SIZE); // grow downward
  t->stack_limit -= GROW_SIZE;
  release(&t->stack_lock);
  resume_execution(t);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to prevent double-growth when two faults race on the guard page?
- What testing harness would you use to verify correctness and latency under contention?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical synchronization primitives in a kernel-like setting, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Wait queues, locks, memory ordering
- Spurious wakeups and rechecking condition
- Correct wakeup under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct waiter { struct waiter *next; task_t *t; } waiter_t;

typedef struct wq { spinlock_t lock; waiter_t *head; bool *flag; } wq_t;

void wait_on(wq_t *q, bool *flag){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  while (!*flag){
    enqueue(q, current); // add to wait list
    release(&q->lock);
    schedule();
    acquire(&q->lock);
  }
  release(&q->lock);
}

void wake_up(wq_t *q){
  acquire(&q->lock);
  *q->flag = true;
  wake_all(q); // wake all waiters
  release(&q->lock);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to handle timeouts?
- How to prevent missed wakeups when wake happens before wait is enqueued?
- Compare to condition variables vs. spin-wait approaches.

## Why This Is Asked

Beginners should reason about virtual memory protection, per-thread isolation, and fault handling without requiring full OS support. Guard pages are a simple, practical mechanism to detect overflows early.

## Key Concepts

- Virtual memory protection and per-thread stacks
- Guard page placement and page faults
- Graceful thread termination and resource cleanup
- Basic testing for concurrency and fault paths

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal skeleton
typedef struct { void *stack; void *guard; } Thread;

void thread_init(Thread *t) {
  t->stack = mmap(NULL, 8*1024*1024, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
                  MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
  t->guard = (char*)t->stack - PAGE_SIZE;
  mprotect(t->guard, PAGE_SIZE, PROT_NONE);
}

void fault_handler(void *addr, Thread *t) {
  if (addr >= t->guard && addr < (char*)t->guard + PAGE_SIZE) {
    terminate_thread(t);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to optionally grow the stack on demand?
- What testing strategy ensures no collateral damage when multiple threads fault simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
Exposes real-world lock-free design pitfalls: memory ordering, ABA, and visibility in a high-throughput ring buffer.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free data structures and CAS
- Per-slot sequencing to avoid ABA
- Memory ordering on x86_64 vs ARM64
- Correctness under contention and wrap-around

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal enqueue (producer)
bool enqueue(slot_t* buf, int n, uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
  static thread_local uint64_t prod = 0; // per-producer sequence
  uint64_t idx = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1) % n;
  slot_t* s = &buf[idx];
  s->key = key;
  s->val = val;
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, prod*2+1, memory_order_release);
  prod++;
  return true;
}

// Minimal dequeue (consumer)
bool dequeue(slot_t* buf, int n, uint64_t* out_key, uint64_t* out_val) {
  static uint64_t tail = 0;
  slot_t* s = &buf[tail % n];
  uint64_t seq = atomic_load_acquire(&s->seq);
  if ((seq & 1) == 0) return false; // empty
  *out_key = s->key;
  *out_val = s->val;
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, seq+1, memory_order_release);
  tail++;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to multiple consumer threads?
- How to test for ABA and late-arriving writes under high contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of NUMA-aware memory management, cross-tenant isolation, and high-throughput reclaim under pressure. It requires concrete data-structures and synchronization strategies rather than high-level talk.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA-aware reclamation and page migration
- Per-container quotas (memcg-like) and accounting
- Eviction policies with hot/not-hot page discrimination
- CAS-based synchronization to avoid double-eviction
- Reclaim throttling to prevent stalls

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal eviction decision sketch
struct page *select_victim(struct mem_node *nodes, int node_count) {
  struct page *victim = NULL;
  int best_score = INT_MAX;
  for (int i = 0; i < node_count; i++) {
    // scan candidate pages on node i, prefer non-hot, remote pages
    struct page *p = first_candidate_on_node(nodes[i]);
    if (!p) continue;
    int score = score_page(p); // lower = better to evict
    if (score < best_score) { victim = p; best_score = score; }
  }
  return victim;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate no thrash with mixed NUMA workloads?
- What metrics would you expose to operators to tune quotas and reclaim rate?

